;; EXPECT: ok
;; LAZY_AC_EQS: on
;; Book chapter 11: lazy AC congruence closure.
;;
;; Same program again, a third flag. `--lazy-ac-eqs` runs saturation plain and pays
;; for completion only when an equality check asks a question plain congruence
;; cannot answer. The search runs inside a semi-persistent transaction: mark the
;; graph, turn completion on, decide, restore. Every node the decision minted is
;; discarded, so the graph you keep is the plain one.
;;
;; That is the whole shape of the mode, and it has one consequence worth stating
;; before you rely on it. The trigger is an EQUALITY CHECK. `(check (= a b))` and
;; `(check (!= a b))` are the only commands that open the transaction, and the
;; first command that is not one of them closes it and restores. So lazy mode
;; answers questions you ask in that form, and it does nothing for a computation
;; that consults equality some other way. Chapter 16 is about exactly one such
;; computation.
;;
;; Consecutive equality checks share one transaction, so a run of them accumulates
;; completion state instead of each rederiving from nothing.

(sort E)
(function add (E) E :assoc :comm)
(function a () E)
(function b () E)
(function c () E)
(function d () E)
(function n () E)

(union (add a b) c)
(union (add a b d) n)

(function f (E) E)
(check (= (f (add a b)) (f c)))

;; Answered by the on-demand search, then rolled back.
(check (= n (add c d)))
